\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Full mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM develops a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional}. Gradient decomposition reveals gradient magnitude $\neq$ causal importance: the reward head (38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$) fails when blocked alone; the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal. Full-pathway SGS (0/3) establishes forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability floor. CIVO is upstream of estimation error---dual critics converge in pure imagination (\S\ref{sec:arch_results})---so standard corrections (Double Q \citep{vanhasselt2016double}, real-data mixing, uncertainty penalization) are inapplicable; MPC succeeds only because re-planning from real states prevents accumulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
Monitor via NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.953$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}). Freezing the $\dt$-embedding reduces CIVO from sustained collapse (4/4 trained) to at most a transient spike (1/4 frozen; NVE $0.19$--$1.71\times$ vs.\ $1.53$--$70.6\times$). Recovery is severity-dependent: moderate CIVO self-corrects (exp\_045, $94\%$ baseline); extreme CIVO (exp\_048, NVE $70.6\times$) leaves irrecoverable damage despite $96\%$ NVE reduction (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}).

\paragraph{Implications for robot learning.}
CIVO poses heightened risk on real hardware \citep{wu2023daydreamer}. Our ManiSkill results reveal three diagnostic modes: (i)~environment transients (StackCube-v1, trained$\approx$frozen), (ii)~mixed-mechanism CIVO (PegInsertionSide-v1: shared onset at ${\sim}52.5$K, divergence at 82.5K with NVE $10.05\times$ vs.\ $1.82\times$; exp\_053 preliminary), and (iii)~pure embedding-driven CIVO (locomotion). Frozen-embedding controls are necessary on each new task.

